# DOES NOT RUN on this node. Kept for the record, and because the numbers it
# printed before dying are worth having -- see the bottom of this comment.
#
# Inferred, not observed: results/6 ran the non-speculative twin of this
# profile, which differs from it by one flag that has nothing to do with the
# kernels below. Speculation is not what breaks; the layout is.
#
# Observed 2026-08-17, results/6, vLLM 0.27.1, 8x H200. The engine loads the
# weights and sizes its KV cache, then dies during CUDA graph capture:
#
#   Capturing CUDA graphs (decode, FULL):   0%|          | 0/19 [00:02<?, ?it/s]
#   (Worker_DP0_TP0_EP0) RuntimeError: Worker failed with error 'Assertion error
#     (/workspace/.deps/flashmla-src/csrc/sm90/decode/sparse_fp8/instantiations/
#      ../splitkv_mla.cuh:742): Assertion `res == CUresult::CUDA_SUCCESS` failed.'
#   (Worker_DP0_TP1_EP1) RuntimeError: Worker failed with error 'CUDA driver
#     error (/workspace/.deps/deepgemm-src/csrc/apis/../jit_kernels/impls/
#      runtime_utils.hpp:144): 700 (CUDA_ERROR_ILLEGAL_ADDRESS)'
#   RuntimeError: Engine core initialization failed.
#
# Both appear in the same run: one worker's illegal access poisons the context
# and the other kernel reports the fallout, so which is cause and which is
# symptom is not settled here. Both are DeepSeek-V4 fused kernels -- FlashMLA's
# sparse-FP8 MLA decode and DeepGEMM's blockscale GEMM -- and both are reached
# only at TP < 8. The recipe pins TP8/DP1 and that is the layout its kernel
# stack is built for; `instantiations/` in the FlashMLA path is a compiled set
# of shapes, and TP2 puts 64 attention heads and 32 indexer heads on a rank
# where TP8 puts 16 and 8.
#
# Not a memory problem. It got as far as reporting, per DP rank:
#   weights 113.6 GiB/GPU, KV 10.64 GiB/GPU, pool 213,076 tokens
# which is 852,304 tokens node-wide against TP8/DP1's 380,117 -- 2.2x the KV
# capacity, and the reason this layout was worth trying at all.
#
# Untested workaround: --compilation-config {"mode": 0, "cudagraph_mode": "NONE"}
# skips the capture that both errors surface in. If that loads, the layout works
# and only graph capture is broken -- but then TP8/DP1 has to be measured the
# same way for the comparison to mean anything.
#
# DeepSeek-V4-Pro split 2-way tensor parallel across 4 data-parallel ranks --
# the far end of the trade deepseek_v4_tp4dp2_speculative describes, and close
# to the limit of it: TP1/DP8 does not fit at all, because a full 28.2 GiB of
# dense weight
# per GPU on top of a 100.4 GiB expert shard overruns a 141 GB card.
#
# Estimated node-wide KV ~33.4 GiB, only ~8% more than tp4dp2, for half the
# per-GPU headroom (8.4 GiB against 15.4). The margin against activation spikes
# at long ISL is what you are spending; this profile exists to find out whether
# that buys anything, and it is the one most likely to OOM.
#
# Estimated, not measured. Run it and read the pool off the serve log.

PROFILE_BASE=deepseek_v4_tp8dp1_speculative

TP_SIZE=2
DP_SIZE=4
